A sender and a receiver that speak a binary protocol I designed, carrying simulated
GPS and IMU readings over UDP. It exists to be the opposite of a project built on a
framework: every byte on the wire is placed deliberately, and everything that would
normally be a library call is written out.

## The wire format

Twenty-eight bytes, fixed layout:

| Field | Bytes | Purpose |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `magic` | 2 | Identifies the protocol; anything else is discarded |
| `version` | 1 | Format version |
| `sensor_type` | 1 | GPS or IMU |
| `sequence_number` | 4 | Monotonic, so order and gaps are recoverable |
| `timestamp` | 4 | Milliseconds since the sender started |
| `values[3]` | 12 | Three float readings |
| `checksum` | 4 | CRC-32 over the preceding 24 bytes |

## The parts worth writing by hand

**CRC-32** is generated rather than linked: a 256-entry lookup table built from the
reflected polynomial `0xEDB88320` on first use, then a byte-at-a-time update over
the payload. Roughly thirty lines, and the reason to write them is that a checksum
you implemented is a checksum you can reason about when a packet fails validation.

**Byte order is explicit everywhere.** Every multi-byte field goes through
`htons`/`htonl` on the way out and `ntohs`/`ntohl` on the way in, so the format is
big-endian on the wire regardless of the machine at either end. Floats are the
interesting case: they are copied into a `uint32_t` with `memcpy`, byte-swapped, and
copied back — never reinterpreted through a pointer cast or a union, both of which
are the usual way to introduce undefined behaviour into a working parser.

**Parsing returns `std::optional<Packet>`,** and a packet earns its way out of that
optional by passing three separate gates: the datagram is exactly 28 bytes, the
magic number matches, and the recomputed CRC agrees with the one carried in the
packet. Anything else is reported and dropped — a receiver on an open socket should
treat every datagram as hostile until it has proved otherwise.

The build is CMake with the parsing and checksum code in a static library shared by
both the sender and receiver executables, so the protocol implementation is compiled
once and cannot drift between the two ends.

## Where it is

The format carries `sequence_number` and the receiver parses and reports it, but
gap and reorder detection is not implemented yet — that is the next piece, along
with a proper test suite for the codec.

